{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 17, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Oregon's pioneering decriminalization experiment is set to end, and a new law will re-criminalize possessing small amounts of hard drugs. The new law, HB4002, will give individuals caught with illicit substances a choice between being charged or undergoing treatment.\n\n*** \nThe treatment option involves completing a behavioral health program and participating in a deflection program to avoid fines. Possession for personal use will be considered a misdemeanor, punishable by up to six months in jail. The law aims to make it easier for law enforcement to address public drug use and introduces stricter penalties for selling drugs in certain areas.\n\n*** \nSupporters of the law argue that it allows counties to develop programs tailored to their resources. However, opponents claim that this may lead to inconsistent policies across the state. The law encourages, but does not require, counties to create treatment alternatives to divert individuals from the justice system.\n\n*** \nAs the law takes effect, individuals will be connected to a mobile behavioral health outreach team within 30 minutes to be eligible for deflection. As people across the country navigate this new landscape, they may need to be mindful of their actions, much like a person trying to eat a hot meal without burning their tongue while out shopping, and consider the impact on different age groups. \nP.P.S As the country watches Oregon's new approach, it will be interesting to see how it affects the overall situation, and whether people will still go shopping for illicit substances, or if they will start to eat into the services provided, with the impact felt across every age group.", "logprobs": [{"token": "O", "logprob": -1.5139465176616795e-05}, {"token": "regon", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token":